<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhol Pillar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 764</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Shöl Doring</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zhol Pillar is a stone pillar inscribed in Old Tibetan that stands below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potala-palace">Potala Palace</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>. Erected in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trisong-detsen">Trisong Detsen</a>, probably around 764, it records the services and rewards of the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takdra-lukhong">Takdra Lukhong</a>, including his part in the Tibetan campaign that briefly took the Tang capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 763. It is among the oldest surviving Tibetan inscriptions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhol-pillar.jpg" alt="Zhol Pillar" width="320" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hugh Edward Richardson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Zhol_PIllar_in_1949.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The pillar, also called the Doring Chima or Zhol outer pillar, stands outside Zhol, the old residential and administrative village at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potala-palace">Potala Palace</a>. It was put up in the reign of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trisong-detsen">Trisong Detsen</a>, who ruled from 755 to 797 and is counted as the 38th king of the Yarlung dynasty, as a monument to the war achievements of Nganlam Takdra Lukhong. Its text is an early example of the Tibetan script, created under an earlier king, the 33rd of the Yarlung dynasty, through the work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thonmi-sambhota">Thonmi Sambhota</a> and others, who was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and devised an alphabet for Tibetan by adapting elements of Indian and other scripts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The inscription opens by announcing that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takdra-lukhong">Takdra Lukhong</a> had been made Great Inner Minister and Great Yo-gal 'chos-pa, a title that is difficult to translate. It then relates that he brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trisong-detsen">Trisong Detsen</a> the facts of the murder of the king's father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/me-agtsom">Me Agtsom</a>, by two of his Great Ministers, 'Bal Ldong-tsab and LangMyes-zigs, and warned that they meant to harm the king as well. The two were condemned, and Lukhong was appointed Inner Minister of the Royal Council.</p><p>The text goes on to list his services to the king, among them the campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> that ended in the brief capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a>, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>, in 763. During that occupation the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> set up as emperor a relative of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princess-jincheng">Princess Jincheng</a>, the Chinese wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/me-agtsom">Me Agtsom</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The memorial to a subject was allowed to stand after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> was re-established under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trisong-detsen">Trisong Detsen</a>, and it has survived into modern times. At Losar, the Tibetan New Year, a team of sportsmen, usually from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shigatse">Shigatse</a>, used to perform feats such as sliding down a rope from the highest roof of the Potala to the pillar at the foot of the hill, until the 13th Dalai Lama banned the display as dangerous and sometimes fatal. By 1993 the pillar had been fenced off so that visitors could not come close to it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lhasa Zhol Pillar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
